\f1\fs22 Chanta\'92s jaw ached in a way that mathematicians in better universes had developed entire branches of non-Euclidean geometry to describe. It was as if it was attached to head-sized planet of earthquakes, lightning strikes, and volcanic eruptions. His eyeballs boiled. His hair was a forest of sharp needles injecting acid into his ossified skull\'97a feature not advertised for any of the FloorMart hair care products he\'92d recently purchased. His ribs felt as cold as prison bars. They constricted lungs and heart. Slithering eels filled his abdomen, seething for a way out.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He vomited.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sir?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta winced at the cacophonous volume of the voice, and scrunched his eyes tight. In their sockets, they felt like overcooked dumplings.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sir? Shall I call you a hover-copt?\'94 The feminine voice impaled a dying part of his brain. Everything was ferocious bright light. Chanta drew a deep breath and held his diaphragm rigid against his spasming stomach. The air smelled like a cosmetics counter in an exclusive department store. Purified essential oils clashed in his nostrils with vapours of eel slime. He threw up again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>His eyebrows felt entangled with his eyelashes. He frowned and blinked to straighten them out. It didn\'92t work. The tiled floor on which he sprawled rose up and smacked him in the head. Walls closed around him. The ceiling came down. He cradled his head in one hand, but that only sent it in a spin.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It must have been quite a party,\'94 the voice said. An elegant sales assistant resolved in Chanta\'92s vision then dissolved again in a swirl of angry bright colours. As his blurring vision returned in low-res, the sales assistant found a fake-crystal bottle and sprayed too much of its contents over him. Overcome with scents of flowers, spices, timbers and hints of toilet cleaner, he almost barfed again as the cologne-pretty assistant mopped at the stains on his expensive, bespoke dinner jacket.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m sorry. I\'92ve made such a mess,\'94 Chanta mumbled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The sales assistant froze. A drunk sleeping it off behind a cosmetics counter in a department store was not an uncommon occurrence\'97standard security systems above the Layer recognised Airborne high fashion and gave it a free pass to almost anywhere, any time\'97but apologising about it certainly was.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta experimented with sitting up. He swallowed hard and managed to keep the eels down. \'93She\'92s married,\'94 he croaked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You must be very proud,\'94 said the assistant, confused. Airborne don\'92t marry; they enter into joint venture agreements.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93To my best friend.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The assistant nodded, relieved. At least that sounded normal; holy joint ventures could be all kinds of nepotism and incest.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I think I love her.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Now the assistant was terrified. \'93I\'92ll call you a hover-copt.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thank you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Please don\'92t thank me. You\'92re scaring me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m sorry.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And stop apologising.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta passed out in the luxurious rear lounge of the pilotless hover-copt while it sat on the pad, confused. Deep in the core of its generative artificial mediocrity, an algorithm ran trying to anticipate Chanta\'92s destination, but most of the possibilities were inconsistent with his dinner jacket. More than one of the destinations\'97a seedy bar in the seediest of bar districts\'97had been recently blown up. After evaluating a series of red flags and alerts, the algorithm selected the most probable of the appalling addresses. It launched, banked and turned, narrowly missing a collision with a piloted craft that was trying to beat another to the vacated pad.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After a short flight, the hover-copt landed with a thud it calculated as minimally sufficient to jolt its passenger awake. It worked. A bleary-eyed Chanta peered out the crystal-bright window. \'94S\'92different,\'94 he mumbled, wondering why he wasn\'92t waking up in a train or an unroadworthy ground car. He shrugged, heavily alighted the vehicle, and staggered to the elevator foyer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>If there was one thing his chemically compromised brain could do after a big night out on the turps, it was guiding him home semi-safely. This Sunday morning was no exception. So, unremarkably, Chanta staggered to his apartment building, up the stairs and to his front door, moving with all the grace of a giraffe attempting ballet on a moving escalator.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the grimy corridor, he leaned against the wall and presented his armpit to the door lock. It clicked a kind of a tut-tut, and flashed red. Chanta groaned. The security system was always fritzing so, like most of the residents, he had a back-up plan. His was simple: climb out the window in the stair well, and traverse around to his always unlocked living room window. The building\'92s craggy face had pockmarks big enough to gain relatively safe toeholds. Chanta knew a path by heart that would take him to his window. He used the same route a dozen times, often with worse hangovers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The window would not open. For a number of moments Chanta tried to process. A futile fury overtook him, and he leant more strength to sliding the window aside.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It can\'92t be locked,\'94 Chanta said to himself for weak reassurance. \'93I\'85 never\'85 lock\'85\'94 He grunted with effort.  \'93this\'85 taxing\'85\'94 He bashed the window with the side of his fist, shifting his centre of gravity so far he lost a toehold. His impaired sense of balance made his overcompensate; his arm swung out over his dangling leg. Too fast, he clutched at the wall, and lost his other toehold.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s ankle took the worst of the initial impact as he hit the pavement. A calf bone cracked with a sound that orthopaedic surgeons recognise as \'94job security\'94. His momentum started to roll his torso. His hip collided next, followed by an elbow, a shoulder, and the side of his skull, each impact producing a unique note in what an avant-garde composer might title 
\f2\i Concerto for Concrete and Body Parts in B Sharp Major
\f1\i0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta let out a wail and a moan. He puffed heavily and waited to see if the pain would get any worse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, my tax! Are you all right?\'94 A concerned bystander rushed to Chanta and loomed over.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta felt for his leg with the one arm that didn\'92t feel on fire. He gave his calf a squeeze. Regretted it. Cried out, and the cry became a guttural roar.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bystander reached out and touched Chanta\'92s shoulder, the one that didn\'92t appear demolished. \'93Should I call you an ambulance?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s eyes flashed wide with fear. \'93Tax, no! No more hospitals.\'94 With the one good arm he crookedly sat up, then winced as his hip complained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bystander recoiled a little, wondering if falling off buildings was a common event for this lunatic, perhaps some sort of extreme hobby like base jumping but with fewer parachutes and more concussions. Chanta waved her closer. \'94Help me up.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What were you doing up there, anyway?\'94 the bystander asked as Chanta hooked his arm over her shoulder, treating her to a close-up olfactory experience that would haunt her for weeks to come.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Trying to get into my apartment. The security on the door is taxed again.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bystander pulled away and regarded Chanta with some disdain. \'93Use your fancy jacket, you dumb prick,\'94 she said as she pulled away and fled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It took Chanta a good three minutes to stand. Precariously he teetered with his weight on one leg, and was about to set off for the apartment building entrance when a kid called out from behind. \'94Excuse me, mister. I think this fell out of your pocket.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Huh?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The kid handed Chanta a small, grey object\'97a tablet thumb drive. Chanta was about to deny it was his, but a dim instinct told him to pocket it. \'94Thanks,\'94 he said to the kid, who regarded him quizzically as he stumbled away. When the kid realised he\'92d be getting no reward for his kindness, he ran at Chanta and pushed him mightily in the lower back and sent him sprawling. \'93Airborne tightwad!\'94 the kid cursed, and scampered off.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>No one else came to help. Everyone was crossing the road to pass by on the other side of the street. It took Chanta a good three minutes to stand precariously, and another quarter of an hour to hop on one good leg, and pull himself up the stairwell handrail with one good arm, before his shadow fell once again on his front door. He regarded the lock mechanism with some hatred as he cursed his own hangover-induced stupidity at forgetting he was wearing the keys to the kingdom on his own back. While very few Cloudborne locks were of the advanced fashion recognition kind, they still possessed basic image recognition for any sufficiently accredited designer label. All Chanta needed was to present the label stitched to the inside pocket of his jacket and his front door lock would flash a happy welcoming green.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Which it did. Chanta apprehensively hopped down the hall, using the wall for support, his vision blurred by the need to pass out. In the living area he almost tripped over an out-of-place dining chair, and kicked the lounge which seemed to block his passage to the bedroom, as if the furniture had been rearranged by someone with strong opinions about feng shui and a personal grudge.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He fumbled with the bedroom door handle and wondered why the door would be closed. With a huge sigh of relief, he toppled onto the bed and before he passed out, he sighed with pleasure at how soft his bed felt, and how fragrantly new it smelled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Some hours later, Chanta woke from a dead sleep to the sounds of screaming.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Get out of my bed! Get out of my house!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Wha\'85? This is\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Get out, or I\'92ll beat your head in!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Too late, Chanta realised the blur coming at him at traumatic speed was the head of a tennis racket. He turned just enough for it to land on his shoulder rather than his skull. He leapt off the bed. With more clarity than he\'92d possessed all day, his took in his surroundings as he fled the bedroom. There was a reason the bed was newer and fresher, and the couch bulkier, and the dining chair in a place he\'92d never left it\'85 wait a minute, he never owned proper dining chairs, considering them to be unnecessary encouragements for people to sit and eat rather than standing near the stasis cupboard like nature intended.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>His whole apartment was fresher. There was new paint, cleaner carpet, bright knock-off art on the wall matched perfectly to the curtains.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He\'92d never thought of it before this moment of escape. He hadn\'92t been back to his apartment in months, not since the night he and Sarah had fled Agent Jones, the night of the Vegas Show.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>If he\'92d given it even the merest thought, he should\'92ve expected it: he\'92d been evicted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And so he ran. He ran with a freedom he had no right to enjoy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He was already healing.
